title: configure(token/url) after the first request keeps using the old credentials
updated: 2026-10-13T19:50:24Z
---

`_forgejo` (`lib/Net/Async/Forgejo/API.pm:68-74`) caches the internal `WWW::Forgejo` in `$self->{_forgejo}`; `configure` (`API.pm:21-28`) stores a new `url`/`token` but never drops that cache. After the first request (or any call that touched `_forgejo`) a reconfiguration changes what the accessors report, not what is sent:

    my $f = Net::Async::Forgejo::API->new(url => 'https://src.ci', token => 'old');
    $f->_forgejo;                                   # any request does this
    $f->configure(token => 'new', url => 'https://other.example');
    print $f->token;                 # new
    print $f->_forgejo->token;       # old   <- goes into the Authorization header
    print $f->_forgejo->base_url;    # https://src.ci/api/v1

`configure` is IO::Async::Notifier's public reconfiguration API, so token rotation on a long-lived notifier silently keeps the revoked token.

Done when: `configure` deletes `$self->{_forgejo}` whenever `url` or `token` is passed (requests in flight keep their old client), with a regression test.

